"The patient underwent robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT2, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. The preoperative PSA was 6.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2). The absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, and nodal sampling was not performed, which widens uncertainty. Biopsy results showed Gleason 3+3 in both sessions, with no evidence of cribriform pattern or intraductal carcinoma.\n\nFactors driving prognosis include the positive surgical margins and the higher preoperative PSA. Countervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the lack of extraprostatic extension. The biopsy findings, showing Gleason 3+3, contrast with the surgical grade of 3+4. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory remain unknown due to missing follow-up documentation. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of nodal status. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial to revise the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 76.42 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.85–106.99 months; its coverage has not been validated."
